GitHub Issue:
Post Date: 2018-03-31T03:43:40Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: [RxJava 2.1] Executing blocking operations on IO scheduler switches execution to a single thread
Description: RxJava 2.1.3

Sample that demonstrates the problem:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

import io.reactivex.Observable;
import io.reactivex.ObservableTransformer;
import io.reactivex.functions.Action;
import io.reactivex.schedulers.Schedulers;

public class Main {
  private static final int NUM_THREADS = 8;
  private static final int NUM_EVENTS = 200;

  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    System.out.println("Executing blocking operation...");
    observe(() -> Thread.sleep((long) (Math.random() * 100)));

    System.out.println("Executing non-blocking operation...");
    observe(() -> {});
  }

  private static void observe(Action op) {
    AtomicInteger messageReceived = new AtomicInteger(0);
    AtomicInteger observableComplete = new AtomicInteger(0);
    ConcurrentHashMap<String, AtomicInteger> counters = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    createObservable()
        .compose(createThreadPoolTransformer())
        .compose(performOperation(op))
        .subscribe(
            e -> {
              counters.compute(Thread.currentThread().getName(), (key, ai) -> {
                ai = ai == null ? new AtomicInteger(0) : ai;
                ai.incrementAndGet();
                return ai;
              });
              messageReceived.incrementAndGet();

              if (messageReceived.get() == NUM_EVENTS) {
                System.out.println(String.format("Counts per thread: %s \n", counters));
              }
            }, e -> {}, observableComplete::incrementAndGet);

    while(observableComplete.get() == 0) {
      // wait until it's complete
    }
  }

  private static Observable<Long> createObservable() {
    return Observable.just(0).repeat(NUM_EVENTS).map(al -> {
      Thread.sleep(10);
      return System.currentTimeMillis();
    });
  }

  private static ObservableTransformer<Long, Long> createThreadPoolTransformer() {
    AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong(0);
    return upstream ->
        upstream
            .groupBy(e -> counter.getAndIncrement() % NUM_THREADS)
            .flatMap(g -> g.observeOn(Schedulers.io()));
  }

  private static ObservableTransformer<Long, Long> performOperation(Action op) {
    return upstream -> upstream
        .map(
            e -> {
              if (Math.random() < 0.5) {
                op.run();
              }
              return e;
            });
  }
}
```
Running the program should print something like:
```
Executing blocking operation...
Counts per thread: {RxCachedThreadScheduler-1=200} 

Executing non-blocking operation...
Counts per thread: {RxCachedThreadScheduler-1=25, RxCachedThreadScheduler-2=25, RxCachedThreadScheduler-3=25, RxCachedThreadScheduler-8=25, RxCachedThreadScheduler-4=25, RxCachedThreadScheduler-5=25, RxCachedThreadScheduler-6=25, RxCachedThreadScheduler-7=25} 
```

It shows that after scheduling execution of blocking operations on an IO pool, the observable runs all these operations on a single thread instead.

I would really appreciate any suggestions to retain equal load on all threads even in the presence of blocking operations!

